cff-version: 1.2.0
title: conceptual-layer
message: >-
  If you use this software or its measurements, please cite it, and cite the
  1994 report the language comes from.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Goodchild
    given-names: Andrew
repository-code: https://github.com/andrewgoodchild/conceptual-layer
abstract: >-
  A conceptual layer: a database's schema grounded in its data and described
  in sentences, ready for an agent to read. It is kept as an ORM model drafted
  from the catalogue and the rows (keys, references, value domains,
  identifiers, the schema inside JSON columns, what profiling the rows finds),
  described to a query writer in its own sentences or in relational terms, and
  served to agents over the Model Context Protocol; with an optional compiler
  from ConQuer-92, H. A. Proper's 1994 conceptual query language, to SQL for
  SQLite and PostgreSQL; and the pre-registered measurements of what each is
  worth to an LLM writing queries. The model's sentences helped where they
  carried what the DDL does not -- definitions the business has written down
  (about +8 points on BIRD, in either language, and as much given to a SQL
  writer as plain English) and what identifies a thing or sits inside a
  document. ConQuer gained nothing measurable over SQL, at more tokens. On
  schemas of about 980 columns, four rounds found nothing -- the layer's
  descriptions, checked derivations, or the benchmark's own column descriptions
  -- that beat a DDL narrowed to the question, for agents that can query the
  data.
keywords:
  - text-to-SQL
  - object-role modeling
  - conceptual query language
  - data reverse engineering
  - data profiling
license: Apache-2.0
# The sources the repository implements, tests against, measures on or uses data
# from, and the few its explanation leans on; each entry's notes say where it is
# used. docs/08-history.md carries its own citations for the lineage it traces.
# NOTICE records the terms of the material that is fetched rather than distributed.
# Entries known only by author, venue and year are cited that way in the work that
# relies on them; their full titles were not recorded and are not guessed.
references:
  # --- The language -----------------------------------------------------------
  - type: report
    title: ConQuer-92 — The revised report on the conceptual query language LISA-D
    authors:
      - family-names: Proper
        given-names: H. A.
    year: 1994
    institution:
      name: Asymetrix Research Laboratory, University of Queensland
    number: Asymetrix Report 94-5
    url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.11926
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2105.11926"
    license: CC-BY-4.0
    notes: >-
      The language the compiler implements: the grammar
      (conquer/conquer-2026-grammar.ebnf), the path-expression semantics and the
      verbalisation rules; model/examples/presidents.ccm.json encodes its
      running example.
  - type: article
    title: >-
      Formal definition of a conceptual language for the description and
      manipulation of information models
    authors:
      - family-names: ter Hofstede
        given-names: A. H. M.
      - family-names: Proper
        given-names: H. A.
      - family-names: van der Weide
        given-names: Th. P.
    journal: Information Systems
    volume: 18
    issue: "7"
    start: 489
    end: 523
    year: 1993
    notes: >-
      LISA-D, which ConQuer-92 restricts and extends ([HPW93] in
      conquer/conquer-2026.md).
  - type: generic
    title: "Rel: A Programming Language for Relational Data"
    authors:
      - family-names: Aref
        given-names: M.
      - name: et al.
    year: 2025
    url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.10323
    identifiers:
      - type: other
        value: "arXiv:2504.10323"
    notes: >-
      Its integrity-constraint counterexamples (§3.5) are the model for
      conquer.py --constraints (conquer/tests/test_constraints.py).
  - type: conference-paper
    title: Constellation Query Language
    authors:
      - family-names: Heath
        given-names: C.
    conference:
      name: OTM 2009
    year: 2009
    notes: >-
      Its verbaliser (activefacts-cql) is what conquer/normalise.py is compared
      with.

  # --- The model: Object-Role Modeling ----------------------------------------
  - type: book
    title: Information Modeling and Relational Databases
    authors:
      - family-names: Halpin
        given-names: T.
      - family-names: Morgan
        given-names: T.
    edition: 2nd
    publisher:
      name: Morgan Kaufmann
    year: 2008
    notes: The ORM the model format follows (model/model.md).
  - type: book
    title: Conceptual Schema and Relational Database Design
    authors:
      - family-names: Halpin
        given-names: T. A.
    edition: 2nd
    publisher:
      name: Prentice-Hall
    year: 1995
    notes: >-
      Rmap, which the reverse engineer inverts (reverse/derive.py); the figure
      behind model/tests/test_abstract.py (p. 402).
  - type: report
    title: ORM 2 Constraint Verbalization
    authors:
      - family-names: Halpin
        given-names: T.
      - family-names: Curland
        given-names: M.
    institution:
      name: Neumont University
    number: ORM2-02
    year: 2006
    notes: >-
      FORML 2; every expected string in model/tests/test_forml.py is quoted
      from it.
  - type: conference-paper
    title: A role calculus for ORM
    authors:
      - family-names: Curland
        given-names: M.
      - family-names: Halpin
        given-names: T.
      - family-names: Stirewalt
        given-names: K.
    conference:
      name: OTM 2009 Workshops
    collection-title: LNCS
    volume: 5872
    start: 692
    end: 701
    year: 2009
    notes: >-
      The role-path level the compiler lowers through (model/model.md §0).
  - type: software
    title: NORMA (Neumont ORM Architect), ORM2Core.xsd
    authors:
      - name: Neumont University
    license: Zlib
    notes: >-
